Description: Tools for dynamic modeling and parameter estimation in ordinary
    differential equation (ODE) models of reaction networks. Models are built
    symbolically and compiled to C/C++ with first- and second-order
    sensitivities. Prediction, observation and objective functions are
    composable function objects that carry symbolic derivatives, following the
    paradigm that derivative information should be used for optimization
    whenever possible. Model calibration uses trust-region optimization with
    optional L1 penalties, multi-start search and profile-likelihood
    identifiability analysis, over experimental conditions that are integrated
    and evaluated in parallel. Further tools cover symbolic steady states,
    parameter transformations with implicit and steady-state solutions, error
    models, and distributed computing on a cluster. Structural identifiability
    is assessed by Lie symmetry detection on the model equations, and the
    symmetries found can be used to reduce a model to an identifiable
    parametrisation. Populations are fitted by nonlinear mixed-effects
    estimation (FOCEI, adaptive sparse-grid Gauss-Hermite quadrature,
    Laplace-EM and stochastic-approximation EM), including L1 penalties that
    cluster individual parameters and select the number of clusters by marginal
    likelihood. Posteriors are sampled by Markov chain Monte Carlo
    (Metropolis-Hastings, Langevin, Hamiltonian and NUTS) and sequential Monte
    Carlo, for single models and for populations. Models, experimental
    conditions, measurements and parameter tables are imported from and
    exported to the PEtab format, with SBML import and export through libSBML.
